## 5. Geometry conditioning is collapsed into a single global latent that is linearly unfolded into the whole sequence

### Code evidence

```python
self.to_tokens = nn.Linear(dmodel, seq_len * dmodel)
...
g = self.encoder(geom_bits)
tokens = self.to_tokens(g).view(-1, self.seq_len, self.dmodel)
return self.decoder(tokens)
```

### Discovery

The encoder output is a single geometry vector, which is then linearly expanded into the entire decoder token sequence.

### Problems

This is a substantial simplification of the intended encoder-decoder interface. The specification proposes a `[GEOM]` token prepended to the frequency sequence, with the decoder conditioned explicitly through cross-attention. R5 instead assumes that one global latent can be unfolded in one shot into all sequence positions. That is computationally convenient, but it weakens the inductive bias for learning structured frequency-specific interactions with geometry.

### Consequence

The model may learn a smooth global mapping from geometry to spectrum, but it is less explicitly equipped to model **frequency-localized geometry effects**, such as notch migration or multi-resonance interference.

